= 1
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For the <XXZ spin chain>, write $\Delta=1+\alpha$ and replace each <spin> by a classical <vector> of length $S$. A bond is $JS^2\mathbf n_m^{\mathsf T}\operatorname{diag}(1,1,\Delta)\mathbf n_{m+1}$, whose minimum is $-JS^2\max(1,|\Delta|)$. For an even periodic chain all bonds can attain that bound simultaneously. The <classical ground states of an XXZ spin chain> therefore give **$\alpha>0$ gives the two easy-axis Néel states** $\mathbf S_m=(-1)^m S\widehat{\mathbf z}$ and its reversal, with energy $-JNS^2(1+\alpha)$.

For **$-2<\alpha<0$ the <ground states> are planar Néel configurations**, $\mathbf S_m=(-1)^m S(\cos\varphi,\sin\varphi,0)$, with arbitrary $\varphi$: their degeneracy is a circle and their energy is $-JNS^2$. This includes the easy-plane interval used later. Part (a), however, does not restrict negative $\alpha$ to that interval. For $\alpha<-2$, $\Delta<-1$ and the lowest states are instead **the two uniform $z$-polarized ferromagnetic configurations**, with energy $JN\Delta S^2$. At $\alpha=-2$, a rotation by $\pi$ about $z$ on alternating sites maps the classical energy to an isotropic <ferromagnet>: $\mathbf S_m=S((-1)^m n_x,(-1)^m n_y,n_z)$ for any <unit vector> $\mathbf n$ is a <ground state>. This special degeneracy is a sphere. At the other boundary, $\alpha=0$, the ordinary Néel direction is arbitrary on a sphere. These statements concern classical minimizing configurations, not the degeneracy of the exact finite-chain quantum <ground state>.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Let $[a,a^\dagger]=1$, $n=a^\dagger a$, and take $S^+=(S^-)^\dagger=\sqrt{2S-n}\,a$. The physical <Holstein–Primakoff occupation constraint> limits the <Hilbert space>, which contains only $n=0,1,\ldots,2S$. Acting in this number basis gives
$$
S^-|n\rangle=\sqrt{(n+1)(2S-n)}|n+1\rangle,\qquad S^+|n\rangle=\sqrt{n(2S-n+1)}|n-1\rangle.
$$
The upper and lower endpoints are annihilated as required. Consequently
$$
[S^+,S^-]|n\rangle=\{(n+1)(2S-n)-n(2S-n+1)\}|n\rangle=2(S-n)|n\rangle=\boxed{2S^z|n\rangle}.
$$
The change in $n$ also gives $[S^z,S^\pm]=\pm S^\pm$. These <matrix elements> prove the <spin> algebra on the full physical spin-$S$ space. Extending the oscillator space to arbitrary occupation would not give a physical finite <spin>: the occupation constraint and operator ordering in the square root matter. <bosons> on distinct sites have $[a_m,a_n^\dagger]=\delta_{mn}$ and all other site <commutators> zero.

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Rotate the <spin> on one sublattice through $\pi$ about its $x$ axis, using the unitary product of the corresponding spin-rotation operators. On a rotated site the old components obey $S^x_{\rm old}=S^x$, $S^y_{\rm old}=-S^y$, $S^z_{\rm old}=-S^z$. Every bond joins opposite sublattices, so
$$
S^x_{m,\rm old}S^x_{m+1,\rm old}+S^y_{m,\rm old}S^y_{m+1,\rm old}=S^x_mS^x_{m+1}-S^y_mS^y_{m+1}=\frac12(S_m^+S_{m+1}^++S_m^-S_{m+1}^-).
$$
The longitudinal product changes sign. Hence **the rotated bond is $-J\Delta S_m^zS_{m+1}^z+\tfrac J2(S_m^+S_{m+1}^++S_m^-S_{m+1}^-)$**, giving the requested form. The canonical <spin> algebra is unchanged because the transformation is unitary. A staggered easy-axis reference configuration is now the uniform all-up state. At $\alpha=0$ one first chooses a Néel direction, here $z$, before performing the same rotation.

= d
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

For occupations small compared with $S$, use $S^z=S-n$, $S^-\simeq\sqrt{2S}\,a^\dagger$ and $S^+\simeq\sqrt{2S}\,a$. The longitudinal bond contributes $-J\Delta S^2+JS\Delta(n_m+n_{m+1})+O(S^0)$; the transverse pair terms contribute $JS(a_ma_{m+1}+a_m^\dagger a_{m+1}^\dagger)+O(S^0)$. Thus the direct real-space expansion is
$$
H_2=-J\Delta NS^2+JS\sum_m\{2\Delta n_m+a_ma_{m+1}+a_m^\dagger a_{m+1}^\dagger\}.
$$
Take $a_m=N^{-1/2}\sum_k e^{ikm}a_k$, with $k=2\pi j/N$ over one full <Brillouin zone> and the lattice spacing set to one in $k$. Symmetrizing $k$ and $-k$ changes the phase in the pair sum to $\cos k$, giving
$$
H_2=-J\Delta NS^2+JS\sum_k\{2\Delta a_k^\dagger a_k+\cos k(a_ka_{-k}+a_k^\dagger a_{-k}^\dagger)\}.
$$
<normal ordering> the second <Nambu spinor> component then yields
$$
\boxed{H_2=-J\Delta NS(S+1)+JS\sum_k(a_k^\dagger\ \ a_{-k})\begin{pmatrix}\Delta&\cos k\\\cos k&\Delta\end{pmatrix}\binom{a_k}{a_{-k}^\dagger}.}
$$
**The printed matrix has a factor-of-two error on its diagonal.** With a full-zone sum its printed $2\Delta$ would produce $4\Delta a_k^\dagger a_k$, and even its vacuum expectation would differ from the direct expansion by $J\Delta NS$. A half-zone convention cannot repair both coefficients: one must then group each $k,-k$ pair and double its off-diagonal pair coefficient as well. The formula above follows from the specified <Hamiltonian> and has the expected gapless isotropic limit; subsequent physical results use this consistent expansion. Terms quartic in <bosons> first enter at order $S^0$.

= e
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

Write $u_k=\cosh\theta_k$, $v_k=\sinh\theta_k$, taking real even $\theta_k$. The transformation gives
$$
[b_k,b_q^\dagger]=(u_ku_q-v_kv_q)\delta_{kq}=\delta_{kq},\qquad [b_k,b_q]=(u_kv_q-v_ku_q)\delta_{k,-q}=0.
$$
The second equality uses $u_{-k}=u_k$ and $v_{-k}=v_k$, while the first uses $u_k^2-v_k^2=1$. The creation-creation <commutator> vanishes by conjugation. Therefore **the $b$ modes are canonical <bosons>**. In the paired basis,
$$
\binom{b_k}{b_{-k}^\dagger}=\begin{pmatrix}u_k&v_k\\v_k&u_k\end{pmatrix}\binom{a_k}{a_{-k}^\dagger}.
$$
Its <determinant> is one and its inverse reverses the signs of the off-diagonal entries. Hence $\boxed{a_k=u_kb_k-v_kb_{-k}^\dagger}$. At self-inverse momenta $k=0,\pi$ this is the corresponding one-mode squeezing transformation, still canonical for finite $\theta_k$.

= f
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=f}

For the <easy-axis XXZ antiferromagnetic spin-wave spectrum>, the consistent quadratic <Hamiltonian> in part (d) has $A=\Delta$ and $B_k=\cos k$. The inverse transformation acts on the Nambu matrix by a congruence. Its diagonal and off-diagonal entries become $A\cosh2\theta_k-B_k\sinh2\theta_k$ and $B_k\cosh2\theta_k-A\sinh2\theta_k$. The pair term vanishes when
$$
\tanh2\theta_k=\frac{\cos k}{\Delta},\qquad \Omega_k=\sqrt{\Delta^2-\cos^2k}.
$$
For $\alpha>0$ the transformation is real and finite for every $k$. Summing the two Nambu <number operators> over the full zone gives
$$
\boxed{H=E_0+\sum_k\epsilon_k(b_k^\dagger b_k+\tfrac12)+O(S^0),\quad E_0=-J\Delta NS(S+1),\quad\epsilon_k=2JS\sqrt{\Delta^2-\cos^2k}.}
$$
Here $E_0$ names the constant in the requested oscillator form; the actual quadratic ground-state energy is $E_{\rm gs}^{(2)}=E_0+\tfrac12\sum_k\epsilon_k=-J\Delta NS^2+JS\sum_k(\Omega_k-\Delta)$. This distinguishes the normal-ordering constant from the total zero-point contribution. <bosonic Bogoliubov diagonalization> is a canonical, not an ordinary unitary, diagonalization of the Nambu matrix.

At $\alpha=0$ and $k\ne0,\pi$, the same formula holds with $\Omega_k=|\sin k|$. The exact <zero modes> have $|\tanh2\theta_k|=1$, so no finite squeezing angle diagonalizes them as positive-frequency oscillators. Treat collective rotations separately or retain a small anisotropy regulator before taking the limit. Finally, if one instead diagonalizes the literal erroneous printed matrix, it gives $\tanh2\theta_k=\cos k/(2\Delta)$ and $\epsilon_k^{\rm print}=2JS\sqrt{4\Delta^2-\cos^2k}$. That result remains gapped at $\alpha=0$ and is not the spin-wave spectrum of the <Hamiltonian> stated at the start.

= g
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=g}

For $\alpha>0$, the dispersion is periodic and has minima at $k=0,\pi$ in the rotated full-zone convention, with
$$
\boxed{\epsilon_{\min}=2JS\sqrt{(1+\alpha)^2-1}=2JS\sqrt{\alpha(2+\alpha)}.}
$$
The maximum is $2JS(1+\alpha)$ at $k=\pm\pi/2$. The easy-axis state has no continuously broken <symmetry>, so a gap is allowed. Near a minimum the energy behaves as $2JS\sqrt{\alpha(2+\alpha)+(\delta k)^2}$. At $\alpha=0$ it becomes **$\epsilon_k=2JS|\sin k|$**, linear near its zeros, with small-$k$ slope $2JS$ in the chosen units. Restoring lattice spacing $a$ and $\hbar$ gives spin-wave speed $2JSa/\hbar$. Zone folding reorganizes the same modes into the magnetic <Brillouin zone>.

\Image[paper-1-spin-wave-spectra.png]
{title=Consistent easy-axis, isotropic and easy-plane linear spin-wave spectra; illustrative anisotropies}

These are linear spin-wave predictions, not a statement that the exact one-dimensional quantum chain always has long-range Néel order. At the isotropic point the zero-point <spin> reduction contains $N^{-1}\sum_k(|\sin k|^{-1}-1)/2$, which diverges logarithmically as the infrared cutoff vanishes. This failure of the assumed ordered reference must not be mistaken for a calculation of the exact interacting spectrum.

= h
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=h}

In this part $0<\Delta=1+\alpha<1$, so the ordered reference lies in the $xy$ plane. Choosing one direction breaks the continuous rotation <symmetry> within that plane. **A gapless, linearly dispersing phase mode is therefore expected** in the spin-wave approximation, while tilting the Néel axis out of the plane costs anisotropy energy. It is incorrect to continue the easy-axis expansion about an unstable $z$-ordered reference and interpret its imaginary frequencies as physical modes.

For the <easy-plane XXZ antiferromagnetic spin-wave spectrum>, take the Néel direction along old $x$, then rotate alternating local <spins> so that all longitudinal components point up. One such local frame gives the full-zone quadratic coefficients
$$
A_k=1+\frac{\Delta-1}{2}\cos k,\qquad B_k=\frac{\Delta+1}{2}\cos k,
$$
and therefore
$$
\boxed{\epsilon_k=2JS\sqrt{(1-\cos k)(1+\Delta\cos k)}.}
$$
Near $k=0$, $\epsilon_k\simeq JS\sqrt{2(1+\Delta)}|k|$. The zone-edge mode has gap $\epsilon_\pi=2JS\sqrt{2(1-\Delta)}$. A different sublattice phase convention shifts the zero by $\pi$; folding into the magnetic zone displays an acoustic and a gapped polarization. The figure uses this explicitly chosen frame. One-dimensional long-wavelength fluctuations again limit the ordered-reference interpretation.

= 2
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Take a uniform nearest-neighbour <harmonic chain> with <displacement> $u_j$, conjugate <momentum> $p_j$, mass $m_0$, <spring> stiffness $\kappa$, lattice spacing $a$ and periodic length $\ell=Na$. These specify a standard model for the requested notes, not additional numerical exam data. Its <Lagrangian> and <Hamiltonian> are
$$
L=\sum_j\left[\frac{m_0}{2}\dot u_j^2-\frac\kappa2(u_{j+1}-u_j)^2\right],\qquad H=\sum_j\left[\frac{p_j^2}{2m_0}+\frac\kappa2(u_{j+1}-u_j)^2\right].
$$
Variation gives $m_0\ddot u_j=\kappa(u_{j+1}-2u_j+u_{j-1})$. For a mode $e^{i(kja-\omega t)}$, periodicity requires $k=2\pi n/\ell$ and
$$
\boxed{\omega_k=2\sqrt{\kappa/m_0}\,|\sin(ka/2)|.}
$$
The lattice makes $k$ periodic modulo $2\pi/a$, so choose one <Brillouin zone>. At long wavelength $\omega_k\simeq v|k|$, where $v=a\sqrt{\kappa/m_0}$ is the <sound speed>. The <continuum limit of a harmonic chain> expands $u_{j+1}-u_j\simeq a\partial_xu$ and replaces $\sum_j$ by $a^{-1}\int dx$. Defining <mass density> $\rho=m_0/a$ and one-dimensional elastic coefficient $K=\kappa a$ gives
$$
L=\int_0^\ell dx\left[\frac\rho2(\partial_tu)^2-\frac K2(\partial_xu)^2\right],\qquad \rho\partial_t^2u-K\partial_x^2u=0,\qquad v^2=K/\rho.
$$
The <momentum> density is $\Pi=\rho\partial_tu$, and the <Hamiltonian> density is $\Pi^2/(2\rho)+K(\partial_xu)^2/2$. Translational invariance of $u$ makes total <momentum> conserved. The continuum approximation describes acoustic wavelengths large compared with $a$; it is not a justification for extending the lattice dispersion and zero-point sums to arbitrarily large <wavevector>.<Applications of Quantum Mechanics by David Tong>[chapter 4, §4.1.1, printed pp.103–105: periodic monatomic chain and acoustic dispersion]{ref}

<canonical quantization> promotes the coordinates and momenta to operators with $[u_j,p_l]=i\hbar\delta_{jl}$ and the other equal-time <commutators> zero. In the continuum, $[u(x),\Pi(x')]=i\hbar\delta_{\rm per}(x-x')$. The nonzero <normal modes> can be represented by independent <phonon> operators obeying $[b_k,b_q^\dagger]=\delta_{kq}$. A useful lattice expansion is
$$
u_j=X+\frac1{\sqrt N}\sum_{k\ne0}\sqrt{\frac{\hbar}{2m_0\omega_k}}\left(b_ke^{ikja}+b_k^\dagger e^{-ikja}\right),
$$
where $X=N^{-1}\sum_j u_j$ is the centre-of-mass <displacement>. The corresponding <momentum> is
$$
p_j=\frac PN-\frac i{\sqrt N}\sum_{k\ne0}\sqrt{\frac{m_0\hbar\omega_k}{2}}\left(b_ke^{ikja}-b_k^\dagger e^{-ikja}\right),\qquad [X,P]=i\hbar.
$$
Including the centre-of-mass term restores the full site <commutator>: the nonzero-mode sum alone gives $i\hbar(\delta_{jl}-1/N)$. Substitution diagonalizes the <Hamiltonian>,
$$
\boxed{H=\frac{P^2}{2Nm_0}+\sum_{k\ne0}\hbar\omega_k(b_k^\dagger b_k+\tfrac12).}
$$
A <phonon> is one quantum of a collective <normal mode>, carrying energy $\hbar\omega_k$ and lattice <momentum> $\hbar k$ modulo a reciprocal-lattice <vector>. Reality of $u_j$ relates its Fourier coordinates by $u_k^\dagger=u_{-k}$; it does not remove the independent oppositely travelling <phonon> modes. Using one complete <Brillouin zone> avoids double-counting real standing-wave coordinates.<Applications of Quantum Mechanics by David Tong>[§4.1.4, quantization of lattice vibrations]{ref}

The free <centre of mass> has no restoring force and must not be treated as an oscillator of zero frequency. Fixing it, weakly pinning it, or specifying a finite centre-of-mass volume defines the corresponding zero-mode factor. With a fixed <centre of mass>, the internal thermal <partition function> and occupations are
$$
Z_{\rm int}=\prod_{k\ne0}\frac1{2\sinh(\beta\hbar\omega_k/2)},\qquad \langle n_k\rangle=\frac1{e^{\beta\hbar\omega_k}-1}.
$$
The internal energy is $\sum_{k\ne0}\hbar\omega_k(\langle n_k\rangle+1/2)$; its high-temperature <heat capacity> approaches $(N-1)k_B$. For a large one-dimensional chain at <temperatures> below the lattice cutoff but above its finite-size mode spacing,
$$
\frac{U_T}{\ell}=\int\frac{dk}{2\pi}\frac{\hbar v|k|}{e^{\beta\hbar v|k|}-1}=\frac{\pi(k_BT)^2}{6\hbar v},\qquad \frac C\ell=\frac{\pi k_B^2T}{3\hbar v}.
$$
This <one-dimensional acoustic phonon heat capacity> shows that **quantization turns classical normal waves into bosonic excitations with a linear low-temperature <heat capacity> in one dimension**. The Euclidean field action $S_E=\tfrac12\int_0^{\beta\hbar}d\tau\,dx\,[\rho(\partial_\tau u)^2+K(\partial_xu)^2]$ is Gaussian, with periodic imaginary-time boundary conditions; its Fourier modes reproduce these oscillators. Absolute <displacement> correlations require the zero-mode convention, and the lattice regulates short-distance fluctuations.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For one bosonic mode, define the <unnormalized bosonic coherent state> $|z\rangle=e^{z a^\dagger}|0\rangle$. The <commutator> $[a,a^\dagger]=1$ gives $a|z\rangle=z|z\rangle$ and $\langle z'|z\rangle=e^{\bar z'z}$. The normalized state is $e^{-|z|^2/2}|z\rangle$; it is not orthogonal to a different <coherent state>. The <coherent-state resolution of identity> follows from Gaussian moments,
$$
\int\frac{d^2z}{\pi}e^{-|z|^2}z^n\bar z^m=n!\delta_{nm},\qquad \boxed{\mathbf1=\int\frac{d^2z}{\pi}e^{-|z|^2}|z\rangle\langle z|.}
$$
For many modes take products of these states and measures. The complex amplitudes have the natural scale of square root occupation; <coherent states> turn normal-ordered <matrix elements> into classical symbols without replacing the quantum <trace> by a single classical configuration.

For <fermions>, $\{c_i,c_j^\dagger\}=\delta_{ij}$ and a nilpotent <annihilation operator> cannot have an arbitrary nonzero ordinary complex <eigenvalue>. Introduce independent <Grassmann variables> $\eta_i,\bar\eta_i$, anticommuting among themselves and with the fermionic operators, and define $|\eta\rangle=\exp(-\sum_i\eta_i c_i^\dagger)|0\rangle$. Then $c_i|\eta\rangle=\eta_i|\eta\rangle$ and $\langle\bar\eta'|\eta\rangle=e^{\sum_i\bar\eta_i'\eta_i}$. With an ordered <Berezin integral> measure satisfying $\int d\bar\eta\,d\eta\,\bar\eta\eta=-1$ for one pair,
$$
\mathbf1=\int D(\bar\eta,\eta)e^{-\bar\eta\eta}|\eta\rangle\langle\bar\eta|,\qquad \operatorname{Tr}O=\int D(\bar\eta,\eta)e^{-\bar\eta\eta}\langle-\bar\eta|O|\eta\rangle
$$
for an even operator $O$; this is the <fermionic coherent-state trace>. In one mode the <trace> integrand is $e^{-\bar\eta\eta}(O_{00}-\bar\eta\eta O_{11})$, whose integral is $O_{00}+O_{11}$. Without the sign reversal it would be the <supertrace>, not the thermal <trace>. These formal Grassmann-valued <coherent states> do not represent ordinary complex-valued <fermion> amplitudes.

To construct the many-body thermal <path integral>, write $\mathcal Z=\operatorname{Tr}e^{-\beta K}$ with $K=H-\mu\hat N$ and divide the imaginary-time interval $\mathcal T=\beta\hbar$ into slices of width $\delta\tau$. Insert the appropriate <coherent-state resolution of identity> between factors. For a normal-ordered <Hamiltonian>, the adjacent-slice kernel is
$$
\langle\bar\psi_{j+1}|e^{-\delta\tau K/\hbar}|\psi_j\rangle=\exp\left[\bar\psi_{j+1}\psi_j-\frac{\delta\tau}{\hbar}K_N(\bar\psi_{j+1},\psi_j)+O(\delta\tau^2)\right].
$$
<coherent-state time slicing> makes the overlap and <Gaussian measure> combine into the first-order time derivative. The <grand-canonical coherent-state action> in the regulated <continuum limit> is
$$
\boxed{\mathcal Z=\int\mathcal D(\bar\psi,\psi)\,e^{-S_E/\hbar},\qquad S_E=\int_0^{\beta\hbar}d\tau\left[\hbar\sum_i\bar\psi_i\partial_\tau\psi_i+H_N(\bar\psi,\psi)-\mu\sum_i\bar\psi_i\psi_i\right].}
$$
For <bosons> the integration variables are complex, with the conjugate labels on the original integration contour; for <fermions> the barred and unbarred <Grassmann variables> are independent. The continuum notation retains the time-sliced ordering prescription. For example, $n(n-1)=a^{\dagger2}a^2$ has normal symbol $\bar z^2z^2$, whereas $n^2$ has that symbol plus $\bar zz$. Dropping that distinction changes a theory. The <coherent-state thermal boundary conditions> follow because the bosonic <trace> closes paths periodically and the fermionic sign in the <trace> closes them antiperiodically:
$$
\psi(\beta\hbar)=\psi(0)\quad\text{(bosons)},\qquad \psi(\beta\hbar)=-\psi(0)\quad\text{(fermions)}.
$$
The barred fields obey the same respective boundary conditions. Hence <Matsubara frequencies> are $2\pi n/(\beta\hbar)$ and $(2n+1)\pi/(\beta\hbar)$. The derivative term encodes the canonical structure; omitting it would remove the quantum dynamics.<Physics 215C: Quantum Field Theory, Part 3 by John McGreevy>[§§3.1–3.2, printed pp.51–61: coherent-state completeness, time slicing and fermionic trace boundary conditions]{ref}

As a check, a free diagonal mode with $K=\xi a^\dagger a$ gives $Z_B=(1-e^{-\beta\xi})^{-1}$ for $\xi>0$, whereas $K=\xi c^\dagger c$ gives $Z_F=1+e^{-\beta\xi}$. Gaussian integration produces an inverse <determinant> for <bosons> and a <determinant> for independent complex <fermions>, with their respective temporal boundary conditions. Differentiating $\log Z$ recovers Bose-Einstein and Fermi-Dirac occupations. Interactions become higher powers in the normal-ordered symbols, and sources generate time-ordered correlations. **The coherent-state construction preserves statistics, ordering and boundary conditions together; a formal continuum expression without its measure and temporal prescription is incomplete.**

= c
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Use the <contact-interaction Bose-gas field integral> for a homogeneous three-dimensional <weakly interacting Bose gas> with repulsive short-range interaction as the standard model. Let particle mass be $m_b$, <scattering length> $a_s>0$, physical coupling $g=4\pi\hbar^2a_s/m_b$ and gas parameter $na_s^3\ll1$. These are symbolic model definitions, not supplied numerical parameters. The grand-canonical coherent-state integral has periodic fields and action
$$
S_E=\int_0^{\beta\hbar}d\tau\int d^3x\left[\bar\psi\left(\hbar\partial_\tau-\frac{\hbar^2\nabla^2}{2m_b}-\mu\right)\psi+\frac g2(\bar\psi\psi)^2\right].
$$
The contact term is the normal symbol of $g\hat\psi^{\dagger2}\hat\psi^2/2$. In three dimensions a <contact interaction> needs a regulator and coupling matched to the measured <scattering length>; the displayed $g$ is the low-energy physical coupling. This field integral is useful because an ordered saddle and its quantum/thermal fluctuations follow from the same action.

Stationarity with respect to $\bar\psi$ gives the imaginary-time <Gross–Pitaevskii equation>. A uniform saddle exists at $\mu>0$ with $\psi_0=\sqrt{n_0}e^{i\theta_0}$ and <condensate density> $n_0=\mu/g$. Its arbitrary phase reflects global $U(1)$ <symmetry>; a selected condensate fixes a phase in the thermodynamic symmetry-breaking description. Write $\psi=\sqrt{n_0}+\eta$ after choosing that phase. Linear terms vanish, and the quadratic part is
$$
S_2=\int d\tau\,d^3x\left[\bar\eta\left(\hbar\partial_\tau-\frac{\hbar^2\nabla^2}{2m_b}+\mu\right)\eta+\frac\mu2(\eta^2+\bar\eta^2)\right].
$$
With $\varepsilon_k=\hbar^2k^2/(2m_b)$ and the Fourier convention $e^{i\mathbf k\cdot\mathbf x-i\omega_n\tau}$, the paired quadratic kernel is
$$
\mathcal M(\mathbf k,\omega_n)=\begin{pmatrix}-i\hbar\omega_n+\varepsilon_k+\mu&\mu\\\mu&i\hbar\omega_n+\varepsilon_k+\mu\end{pmatrix},\qquad \det\mathcal M=(\hbar\omega_n)^2+\varepsilon_k(\varepsilon_k+2\mu).
$$
The one-half in the <Nambu spinor> action removes pair double counting. Analytic continuation of the inverse kernel gives **the Bogoliubov dispersion**
$$
\boxed{E_k=\sqrt{\varepsilon_k(\varepsilon_k+2gn_0)},\qquad E_k\simeq\hbar c_s k,\qquad c_s=\sqrt{gn_0/m_b}.}
$$
At high <momentum> it approaches the free-particle <kinetic energy> plus its mean-field shift. The crossover scale is the <healing length> $\xi=\hbar/\sqrt{2m_bgn_0}$. Gaussian integration adds a fluctuation <determinant> to the saddle <free energy>. Expressed using the physical three-dimensional coupling, its zero-temperature integrand can be regularized as $\tfrac12[E_k-\varepsilon_k-\mu+\mu^2/(2\varepsilon_k)]$; the subtraction follows from <renormalization of a three-dimensional dilute-gas contact coupling>, not discarding an infinite answer arbitrarily. The thermal contribution is $k_BT\int d^3k/(2\pi)^3\log(1-e^{-\beta E_k})$. A calculation beyond mean field must keep that matching and its thermodynamic derivatives consistent.

The <density-phase action of a Bose gas> makes the physical content clear, $\psi=\sqrt{n_0+\delta n}\,e^{i\theta}$. Up to a periodic density total derivative, the long-wavelength quadratic action contains
$$
S_2=\int d\tau\,d^3x\left[i\hbar\delta n\,\partial_\tau\theta+\frac g2(\delta n)^2+\frac{\hbar^2n_0}{2m_b}(\nabla\theta)^2+\frac{\hbar^2}{8m_bn_0}(\nabla\delta n)^2\right].
$$
<number-phase conjugacy> pairs the density fluctuation and phase in the <quadratic density-phase action>. Dropping the density-gradient term only in the long-wave limit and completing the density Gaussian square gives the <Bose-gas phase-only action>
$$
S_{\theta}=\frac12\int d\tau\,d^3x\left[\frac{\hbar^2}{g}(\partial_\tau\theta)^2+\frac{\hbar^2n_0}{m_b}(\nabla\theta)^2\right].
$$
This has the same sound pole. A term $i\hbar n_0\partial_\tau\theta$ is also present before restricting to the smooth zero-winding sector; although locally a total derivative, it cannot in general be ignored in topologically nontrivial sectors. The density and phase variables describe the same coupled Bogoliubov branch, not a second independent massive particle simply because the density has a static restoring cost.<Physics 215C: Quantum Field Theory, Part 3 by John McGreevy>[§2.3, printed pp.29–38: superfluid phase stiffness, amplitude fluctuations and phase dynamics]{ref}

A phase gradient carries superflow $\mathbf v_s=(\hbar/m_b)\nabla\theta$. Its energy cost defines the <superfluid number density>. At leading mean-field order the stiffness uses $n_0$, but <condensate density> and <superfluid number density> are different observables: for a Galilean-invariant continuum gas at zero <temperature> the latter is the full density even when interactions deplete the condensate. Bogoliubov coefficients give $v_k^2=[(\varepsilon_k+gn_0)/E_k-1]/2$ and a small zero-temperature depletion of relative order $\sqrt{na_s^3}$ in three dimensions. Large depletion or strong long-wavelength phase fluctuations would invalidate the small-fluctuation saddle approximation.

The <Landau criterion> for creating excitations is $v_L=\inf_{k>0}E_k/(\hbar k)=c_s$ for this homogeneous dispersion. Thus **repulsion produces a linear sound mode and a positive critical velocity from the <Landau criterion>**, whereas an ideal quadratic gas has zero value of this criterion. Vortices, obstacles and nucleation can reduce an actual flow threshold below $c_s$; the criterion alone is not a complete theory of metastable flow. At finite <temperature> thermal <quasiparticles> reduce the <superfluid number density>. Dimensionality and finite size also matter: the uniform three-dimensional weak-depletion condensate expansion must not be transferred unchanged to a one- or two-dimensional thermodynamic system. The field integral provides systematic corrections only within its dilute, stable and sufficiently weak-fluctuation regime.

= 3
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Keep the electromagnetic units of the supplied <Lagrangian>, including its factor $c$, and regard $e$ as the signed coupling used there. Set $a_B=e\Phi_B/(2\pi c)$. The <canonical momentum> is $p_\phi=I\dot\phi+a_B$, so the <Legendre transform> gives
$$
H=\frac{(p_\phi-a_B)^2}{2I},\qquad \boxed{\hat H=\frac1{2I}\left(-i\hbar\partial_\phi-\frac{e\Phi_B}{2\pi c}\right)^2.}
$$
Single-valued periodic wavefunctions have normalized eigenstates $e^{in\phi}/\sqrt{2\pi}$ with integer $n$. Defining dimensionless flux $f=e\Phi_B/(hc)$ gives $E_n=\hbar^2(n-f)^2/(2I)$, and the <trace> is therefore
$$
\boxed{Z=\sum_{n\in\mathbb Z}\exp\left[-\frac{\beta\hbar^2}{2I}(n-f)^2\right].}
$$
The <flux-threaded quantum rotor> spectrum is periodic under $f\mapsto f+1$ after relabelling $n$, corresponding to flux period $hc/|e|$ in these units. A local gauge change can remove the <magnetic vector potential> from the differential operator but then imposes a <twisted boundary condition> with phase $e^{-2\pi if}$; it cannot remove the flux dependence from both the <Hamiltonian> and boundary condition simultaneously. In SI conventions the flux coupling has no $c$, so one must not mix the two unit systems.

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <trace> integrates the diagonal thermal kernel over a single fundamental interval, $Z=\int_0^{2\pi}d\phi_0\,\langle\phi_0|e^{-\beta\hat H}|\phi_0\rangle$. On the circle a closed path can wind an integer number $m$ of times. Lift it to the real covering coordinate so its endpoints are $\phi(0)=\phi_0$ and $\phi(\mathcal T)=\phi_0+2\pi m$, with $\mathcal T=\hbar\beta$.

Under <Wick rotation> $t=-i\tau$, $\dot\phi=i\partial_\tau\phi$, so the real-time phase $iS/\hbar$ becomes minus the <Euclidean action> divided by $\hbar$, with
$$
\boxed{S_E[\phi]=\int_0^{\mathcal T}d\tau\left[\frac I2(\partial_\tau\phi)^2-i\frac{e\Phi_B}{2\pi c}\partial_\tau\phi\right].}
$$
Thus sum the <path integral> $e^{-S_E/\hbar}$ over these lifted endpoint conditions and integrate $\phi_0$ from zero to $2\pi$. This gives the requested winding-sector representation of the <trace>. The flux term is a total derivative, so it leaves the local classical equation unchanged, but its weight is $e^{i2\pi fm}$ and distinguishes different winding sectors. This is the <Aharonov-Bohm phase> in a thermal <path integral>, rather than a force from a magnetic field on the ring itself.

= c
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The Euclidean saddle in winding sector $m$ is a constant-speed lifted path, $\phi_{\rm cl}(\tau)=\phi_0+2\pi m\tau/\mathcal T$. Write $\phi=\phi_{\rm cl}+\eta$ with Dirichlet fluctuation endpoints $\eta(0)=\eta(\mathcal T)=0$. The cross term in the kinetic action is proportional to $\int\partial_\tau\eta\,d\tau=0$, and the flux term of $\eta$ vanishes for the same reason. Therefore
$$
\frac{S_E}{\hbar}=\frac{2\pi^2I}{\hbar^2\beta}m^2-i2\pi fm+\frac I{2\hbar}\int_0^{\mathcal T}(\partial_\tau\eta)^2d\tau.
$$
Define the fluctuation prefactor by
$$
Z_0=\int_{\eta(0)=\eta(\mathcal T)=0}\mathcal D\eta\,\exp\left[-\frac I{2\hbar}\int_0^{\mathcal T}(\partial_\tau\eta)^2d\tau\right].
$$
It is the free-line diagonal Euclidean propagator for the angular coordinate with inertia $I$, or equivalently the Gaussian bridge amplitude about a given classical lifted path. It is independent of $m,\phi_0$ and flux. It is **not** the complete zero-flux ring <partition function>, which still includes every winding and the initial-angle integral. Translational independence supplies the factor $\int_0^{2\pi}d\phi_0=2\pi$, leaving
$$
\boxed{Z=2\pi Z_0\sum_{m\in\mathbb Z}\exp\left[-\frac{2\pi^2I}{\hbar^2\beta}m^2+i2\pi fm\right].}
$$
The <momentum-winding duality of a quantum rotor> uses a Gaussian expansion that is exact here because the kinetic action is quadratic and the flux coupling is a total derivative. It is not merely a leading semiclassical approximation.

\Image[paper-1-flux-ring-paths.png]
{title=Lifted winding sectors on a flux-threaded ring and the periodic flux dependence of quantum energy levels}

= d
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Continue the <free-particle propagator> to $t=-i\mathcal T$ and replace its coordinate mass by the angular inertia $I$. The <Euclidean free-particle bridge amplitude> at coincident endpoints is
$$
\boxed{Z_0=\sqrt{\frac I{2\pi\hbar\mathcal T}}=\sqrt{\frac I{2\pi\hbar^2\beta}}.}
$$
This normalization includes the time-sliced <Gaussian measure>, not just a formal <determinant> with an unspecified multiplicative constant. Set $A=2\pi^2I/(\hbar^2\beta)>0$. The correct <Poisson summation formula> is $\sum_mF(m)=\sum_n\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}dx\,F(x)e^{-2\pi inx}$; the source PDF omits the integral sign before its $d\theta$. For $F(x)=e^{-Ax^2+i2\pi fx}$ the Gaussian Fourier integral gives
$$
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}dx\,e^{-Ax^2+i2\pi(f-n)x}=\sqrt{\frac\pi A}\,e^{-\pi^2(n-f)^2/A}.
$$
The overall prefactor cancels exactly: $2\pi Z_0\sqrt{\pi/A}=1$, and $\pi^2/A=\beta\hbar^2/(2I)$. Hence
$$
\boxed{Z=\sum_n e^{-\beta\hbar^2(n-f)^2/(2I)},}
$$
in agreement with the angular-momentum <trace>. Pairing $m$ and $-m$ makes the winding expression real, and the <momentum> expression shows positivity. Both are periodic in flux and invariant under $f\mapsto-f$. At high <temperature> the zero-winding term gives $Z\simeq\sqrt{2\pi I/(\beta\hbar^2)}$, with exponentially small flux dependence; at low <temperature> the nearest integer angular-momentum state dominates, with two equal lowest terms at half-integer flux. These independent limits also check the normalization and the flux phase.
